  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed method employs gradient descent with an adaptive learning rate to minimize the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. By iteratively identifying the shift causing the maximum overlap and adjusting h accordingly, while ensuring constraints, it efficiently navigates the solution space. The projection step maintains feasibility, balancing exploration and exploitation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe aim to minimize C subject to:\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. sum(h) = n_points / 2\n3. For each k, overlap C_k = np.correlate(h, 1-h, k) * dx \u2264 C\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization\n- Use initial_h_values if available; else, create a feasible h.\n- Compute n_points and dx.\n\nb. Projection function\n1. Clip h to [0,1]\n2. Adjust sum to n_points / 2 by redistributing excess or deficit.\n\nc. Optimization loop\n- Compute overlaps and identify k_max.\n- Compute gradient as (1 - np.roll(h, k_max)) * dx.\n- Update h with adaptive learning rate and project.\n- Track best solution and decay learning rate on plateau.\n\nd. Termination and validation\n- Check time remaining and validate constraints.\n\n4. Search choices\n- Initial learning rate: 0.1 with decay on plateau.\n- Adapt learning rate with decay factor 0.5 every 100 steps without improvement.\n\n5. Compute budget\n- With n_points=45, each iteration is feasible within 1100s.\n\n6. Validation\n- Ensure h meets constraints and evaluate using the task metric.",
